  <data name="PropDesc_OutputDirectory" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Folder the recordings are written to. Pick one with the "..." button, or type a path. Leave empty to use the folder the executable is in. A relative path is resolved against that same folder. A recorder whose filename template is an absolute path ignores this setting.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="PropDesc_RecordingRetentionDays" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Delete .mp4 files under the output folder once they are older than this many days. 0 deletes nothing. Sub-folders are searched too, and a sub-folder is removed when deleting left it empty. Point the output folder at a directory used only for recordings before turning this on — left empty it covers everything under the folder the executable is in.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="PropDesc_RecordingCleanupIntervalHours" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>How often the deletion runs, in hours. It also runs once right after startup, so a long interval still gets a sweep every time the app starts. Values below 1 are treated as 1, and values above 1000 as 1000.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="PropCat_RemoteControl" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Remote control</value>
  </data>
  <data name="PropDesc_RemoteControlEnabled" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Run a small HTTP server so the app can be operated from a browser on another PC on the same LAN. Off by default. Reading (the recorder list, their state and the settings) needs no token at all, so anything that can reach the port can see it - keep it off unless the network is one you trust. Writing needs the access token. Windows Firewall is not opened for you: allow the port by hand.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="PropDesc_RemoteControlBindAddress" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>IP address to listen on. 0.0.0.0 (the default) means every network interface, which is what "from another PC" needs; 127.0.0.1 restricts it to this machine. Changing this restarts the server.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="PropDesc_RemoteControlPort" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>TCP port to listen on. 0 lets the operating system pick a free port - the port actually taken is written to activity.log as remote.start. Windows Firewall still has to be opened by hand for the port you end up on.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="PropDesc_RemoteControlAccessToken" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Secret required for every write (starting and stopping recordings, changing settings). It is generated when remote control is first turned on; the "..." button makes a new one, which invalidates the old one and any browser session opened with it. Reading is not covered by this token.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="PropDesc_RemoteControlAllowGuestRead" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Allow read-only access without signing in.</value>
  </data>
